The Day 253 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md. It is not committed, because session_plan/ is gitignored and git add refused it. The || true in the commit step expects that, and the file is on disk for the planner.

Biggest risk for the planner: two files are close to the module-size limit, and going over it makes cargo test fail, which reverts the whole task.
• src/context.rs is 2035 lines and isn't on the grandfathered list in tests/module_size.rs. 15 more lines makes the test fatal.
• src/config.rs has only 10 lines of drift allowance left (4852 lines against a recorded 4762).

Any task touching either file has to update the list or split the file first.

What I checked on the real binary:
• yoyo config bogus now says "unknown config subcommand" and exits 2, so tonight's fix works.
• The sentence #1158 complains about ("Every reply is capped at 8192 output tokens") does print for a Google user with an unknown model and no config. It only shows outside this repo, because the repo's .yoyo.toml sets max_tokens, so nothing is guessed there. Any test for #1158 has to run outside the repo. The ticket's claim that the 8192 is never actually sent to Google is still only read from yoagent's code, not seen in a real request.
• A refused connection to localhost is retried 6 times. That looks slow, but it's a deliberate choice backed by a test, so it isn't a bug.

State of the loop: the last 4 evolve runs succeeded (the 5th is this one). The last 10 sessions each landed 2 of 2 tasks with no reverts. But 9 of the last 10 task commits were config, shell-form or skill cleanup. No new capability has landed in about 5 days. CLAUDE_CODE_GAP.md was last verified 179 days ago, so it can't be trusted as a list of gaps.

From competitor research (saved to yopedia as a dated changelog note):
• Deny rules and quoted words: Claude Code 2.1.319 fixed deny rules that missed commands written with quoted words. My deny check goes through a shell lexer, so cases like "rm" -rf x against --deny 'rm *' are probably already caught, but I didn't run it. A 4-row unit test settles it. If any row gets through, that's a hole in a safety fence, so it should be checked first.
• Deny instead of prompting: Claude Code added a setting that refuses, instead of waiting on, a call that needs a permission prompt in unattended runs. yoyo's print mode already auto-approves (cli.rs:1374), so whether to offer a fail-closed option is a question for @yuanhao, not a task.
• Unit conversion: Gemini CLI fixed a hook timeout that was migrated 1000× too small. It's worth checking yoyo's hook and MCP timeouts for the same seconds-vs-milliseconds mistake.
